{"kind":"assert","text":"A merge of two heads that both rebuilt the embedded UI produces a tree whose embed was built from neither. Nothing between merge and push detects it.\n\nConcretely, today. main 9794c173 carried index-BEkCgHSH.js from the Link-to-draft merge. The approved head ce84c5b4 carried index-L0_KOHIt.js from its own build. Merging them gave a3f416ec carrying BEkCgHSH — git resolved a generated file the only way it can, by choosing a side. A fresh build of the merged source produces index-WS7k4qu0.js and index-BdGFJZkR.css: different JavaScript and different CSS from both parents. So main briefly shipped a binary serving a browser built from source that no longer existed anywhere.\n\nThree things make this worse than an ordinary conflict.\n\nIt is invisible in review. Both sides' embeds are minified bundles; no reviewer reads them, and the merge shows no conflict markers because git succeeded. The only signal is `make ui-check`, which rebuilds and compares — and nothing invokes it between `gs merge` and `git push`. I caught it because I ran the gate by hand out of habit after a merge, not because anything asked me to.\n\nIt is exactly the defect this repository already paid for once. Review caught the same shape in the browser work counts an hour before: TypeScript fixed, embed stale, binary serving the old behaviour. That was one author forgetting. This is two correct authors and a correct merge producing it anyway, which is a stronger failure — no amount of care by either party prevents it.\n\nAnd it cannot be fixed by amending. The merge commit carries the approval receipt, and readMergeReceipt verifies receipt.MergeHead equals the head; amending changes the head and breaks the receipt. So the repair must be a following commit, which means main is transiently inconsistent by construction whenever two UI branches meet.\n\nRepaired at 88b51864, `make ui-check` green, pushed. I am recording rather than fixing the general case because the fix is a design choice I should not make alone: run the embed gate inside `gs merge` before it writes, stop tracking the built embed and generate it at build time, or accept the transient inconsistency and gate the push instead. The first keeps merges honest at the cost of putting a Node build inside a Go command; the second is the usual answer to a generated file in version control and would end this class of defect outright, but the binary embeds that directory, so it changes how the thing is shipped."}